.TH RUNUZ 1 "October 2026" "runuz 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
runuz \- standalone filesystem CLI: read, create, and surgically edit files by symbol and structure
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runuz
.I command
[
.I options
]
.sp
.B runuz read \-\-file\-path
.I path
.RI [
.B \-\-symbol
.I addr
.RI |
.B \-\-query
.I name
.RI |
.B \-\-pattern
.I regex
.RI ]
.RI [
.B \-\-json
.RI ]
.br
.B runuz create \-\-file\-path
.I path
.RI [
.B \-\-new\-source
.I text
.RI ]
.br
.B runuz replace \-\-file\-path
.I path
.RI [
.B \-\-symbol
.I addr
.RI |
.B \-\-symbols
.I a,b
.RI ]
.RI [
.B \-\-new\-source
.I text
.RI ]
.br
.B runuz insert_before \-\-file\-path
.I path
.B \-\-symbol
.I addr
.B \-\-new\-source
.IR text
.br
.B runuz insert_after \-\-file\-path
.I path
.B \-\-symbol
.I addr
.B \-\-new\-source
.IR text
.br
.B runuz delete \-\-file\-path
.I path
.RI [
.B \-\-symbol
.I addr
.RI |
.B \-\-symbols
.IR a,b ]
.br
.B runuz rename \-\-file\-path
.I path
.B \-\-symbol
.I name
.B \-\-new\-name
.IR name
.br
.B runuz tools
.RI [
.B \-\-json
.RI ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runuz
is a single binary with one address grammar shared by every edit
operation.  An address names any unit of any file: a symbol in source
code, a key in a config, a heading in a document.  The same address
works for
.BR replace ,
.BR insert ,
.BR delete ,
and
.BR rename .
.PP
Code files are parsed with tree\-sitter.  Every write to a code file is
re\-parsed before it is accepted; a result that does not parse is
rejected and the file is left untouched.  Writes to text files validate
structure where a format exists (JSON stays JSON).
.PP
Run
.B runuz help
or
.B runuz
with no arguments for the short usage text.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B read
Filesystem analysis: discover, study, and search.  Works on any file.
.PP
Output is determined by the file type:
.RS 4
.TP
.B code
STRUCTURE\-ONLY symbol outline: kind, name, and line range per symbol
\-\- no source dumps.  Each symbol is followed by its callees as a
.I \-> name, name
suffix.
.TP
.B config / document
Anchor outline (JSON keys, YAML/TOML/env names, markdown headings).
.TP
.B extensionless
Raw content.
.RE
.PP
At most one modifier:
.RS 4
.TP
.B \-\-symbol
Output just the addressed unit's range.  For code names the range is
prefixed with a
.I calls:
line.
.TP
.B \-\-query
Fuzzy search over symbol NAMES.  Structure listing only, no content.
.TP
.B \-\-pattern
Regular expression over content.  Every hit is annotated with the
smallest enclosing symbol (function, class, ...) when the file is code.
.RE
.TP
.B create
Create a new file.  Fails if the file exists.  Code files are re\-parsed
after writing and a syntax\-error result aborts the create; configs and
documents are written as\-is.  An empty
.B \-\-new\-source
creates an empty file.
.TP
.B replace
Replace an addressed unit, or the whole file when the address is
omitted.  An empty
.B \-\-new\-source
deletes the resolved range.  Code writes are re\-parsed; a broken result
aborts and the file stays untouched.
.TP
.B insert_before
Splice
.B \-\-new\-source
immediately before the anchor address.  Code writes are re\-parsed;
text writes validate structure.
.TP
.B insert_after
Splice
.B \-\-new\-source
immediately after the anchor address.
.TP
.B delete
Delete the addressed unit.  The unit's whole line is removed, including
a field terminator (such as a trailing comma); an adjacent blank line is
eaten only when the line left behind is surely blank, so a sibling
sharing the line survives.
.TP
.B rename
Rename a symbol across a code file (function, struct, class, method,
and so on).
Every word\-boundary occurrence of the name is replaced.  On text
files the word is renamed everywhere.
.TP
.B tools
Advertise the tool surface.  With
.BR \-\-json ,
emit one
.I ToolDef
per command, named
.IR runuz_ <command>,
with the input schema.  This is the contract external agents mirror to
discover the CLI; dispatching is generic
.RI "( runuz <command> \-\-<key> <value> )",
so growing the CLI never requires a consumer change.
.SH ADDRESSING
An address is a single string passed to
.BR \-\-symbol .
There are two forms.
.SS Named symbols (code)
A symbol path is dot\-separated segments:
.sp
.nf
alpha
Server.start
Carrier.hold.when.otherwise
alpha.loop#2.body
imports
.fi
.PP
Named segments resolve against the symbol index.  Symbols are the
named structural definitions a language exposes: functions, methods,
classes/structs/traits/interfaces, impl blocks, enums, type aliases,
modules, consts/statics, and struct fields.
.PP
Control flow is reached with seven alias words:
.RS 4
.TP
.B body
The inside block of any compound (function body, then\-branch, loop
body, try block).
.TP
.B when
An
.B if
.
.TP
.B otherwise
The alternate branch: the
.B else
of an
.BR if ,
or a catch clause of a
.BR try .
.TP
.B loop
A
.BR for ,
.BR while ,
.BR loop ,
or
.BR do .
.TP
.B try
A try construct (unsupported on Rust, see NOTES).
.TP
.B return
A return statement.
.TP
.B call
A function call.
.RE
.PP
The walk is document order.  Occurrences are disambiguated with
.IR #N ;
.N counts distinct siblings, not nested matches inside earlier matches,
so a call inside a call is
.I call#1.call,
not
.IR call#2 .
.PP
A bare name that matches several symbols fails loudly rather than
guessing.  Qualify by containment
.RI ( Type.empty
resolves the method inside the type, even when a free function
.B empty
also exists) or pin an occurrence with
.IR #N .
.PP
The synthetic symbol
.B imports
addresses the leading contiguous run of top\-level import/use/require/
include declarations of any language, as one unit.
.SS Shape rungs (any file)
Four structural rungs address non\-symbol structure on any file,
including text:
.sp
.nf
token <text>       a single word
slot <text>        a format\-aware key
statement <text>   one logical statement or line
block <text>       an indented region
.fi
.PP
A
.B slot
is resolved against the file's format first: a JSON key path
.RI ( auth.api_url ),
a YAML/TOML/env key
.RI ( package.version ,
.RI DATABASE_URL ),
or a markdown heading.  When the text does not match a format element,
the slot falls back to an exact substring search.
.PP
On a plain text file, a bare
.B NAME
address (no shape word) also resolves as an exact substring.
.SH PATH RESOLUTION
.B \-\-file\-path
accepts:
.RS 4
.TP
.B file
An absolute path to a file.
.TP
.B directory
An absolute directory; the tool walks it.
.TP
.B glob
A pattern such as
.I '/src/**/*.ts'
or
.I '/repo/*.rs'.
.RE
.PP
Relative patterns are anchored at the current working directory.
.SH MULTI\-EDIT
.B \-\-symbols a,b
(comma\-separated) applies
.B replace
or
.B delete
to several named code symbols as one atomic edit: all ranges are
resolved against the same source, applied in one pass, and written
once.  Overlapping or adjacent ranges coalesce into a single span.
Any missing name aborts the whole edit; nothing is applied partially.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure.  An error message is printed to stderr.  With
.BR \-\-json ,
the result is a JSON document carrying the error instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Outline a source file
runuz read --file-path src/main.rs

# Show just one symbol, with the functions it calls
runuz read --file-path src/main.rs --symbol Server.start

# Swap a config value by its key
runuz replace --file-path Cargo.toml \\
        --symbol 'slot package.version' \\
        --new-source 'version = "0.4.0"'

# Edit a JSON key path
runuz replace --file-path app.json \\
        --symbol 'slot auth.api_url' \\
        --new-source '"https://prod.example"'

# Replace a function body through the sub-walk
runuz replace --file-path main.rs \\
        --symbol alpha.body \\
        --new-source '{ 42 }'

# Delete two definitions in one atomic edit
runuz delete --file-path main.rs --symbols dead_one,dead_two

# Splice a call after a named anchor
runuz insert_after --file-path main.rs \\
        --symbol Carrier.hold --new-source 'drop_for(&x);'

# Rename a type everywhere it appears
runuz rename --file-path lib.rs --symbol Legacy --new-name Modern

# Search content, annotated with the enclosing function
runuz read --file-path '/repo/**/*.rs' --pattern 'TODO'
.fi
.SH NOTES
The
.B try
alias is unsupported on Rust: tree\-sitter\-rust has no top\-level try
node, and the
.B ?
postfix is a unary operator without a recognizable scope.
.PP
The byte range of a symbol is half\-open
.IR [start,end) ;
row ranges are 1\-based and inclusive on both ends.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR tree (1)